    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.maximal_independent_set - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.maximal_independent_set.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.maximal_independent_set()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find a maximal independent set in the graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.maximal_independent_set()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find a maximal independent set in the graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.maximal_independent_set()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Find a maximal independent set in the graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind a maximal independent set in the graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L85007-L85123){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.maximal_independent_set(\n    nodes=None,\n    seed=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Maximal independent set](../assets/graphs/maximal-independent-set.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A five-node graph: a triangle 2, 3, 5 with pendant nodes 1 and 4. Nodes 2 and 4, in the accent color, share no edge, and every other node is adjacent to one of them, so no node can be added. The card shows the result [2, 4], size 2, smaller than the maximum of 3.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA maximal independent set is an independent set that cannot be extended by adding any other\nvertex. In other words, every vertex not in the set is adjacent to at least one vertex in\nthe set. \"Maximal\" is weaker than \"maximum\": the result cannot grow, but a different\nchoice of nodes might yield a larger set.\n\nThis uses a randomized greedy algorithm: it shuffles the candidate nodes, then walks\nthrough them, adding each node that is not adjacent to a node already chosen. Because the\nshuffle changes from run to run, different calls can return different (equally valid)\nsets unless `seed=` is fixed.\n\nTypical uses include:\n\n- picking a batch of mutually non-conflicting jobs, tasks, or resources\n- selecting well-spread \"representative\" nodes, since every other node is adjacent to one\n- generating many cheap candidate solutions (see `find_maximal_independent_sets()`)\n  when hunting for a large independent set\n\nFor the largest set the library can find, use `maximum_independent_set()`, which uses a\ndeterministic degree-based heuristic. Edge direction is ignored, as in\n`is_independent_set()`: on a directed graph, an edge `A -> B` means `A` and `B` can't\nboth be chosen. Self-loops are ignored.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[nodes]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[int] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional starting set of node IDs that must appear in the result. These nodes must\n    already form an independent set; the algorithm keeps them and extends the set until\n    it is maximal. If `None` (the default), the algorithm starts from an empty set.\n\n<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Random seed controlling the order in which candidate nodes are considered. Passing the\n    same integer always gives the same result on the same graph, which is useful for\n    reproducible analyses and tests. If `None` (the default), the order is drawn from\n    system entropy, so repeated calls may return different sets.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   List of node IDs forming a maximal independent set, sorted in ascending order. An\n    empty graph gives an empty list.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the starting nodes don't form an independent set (have adjacent nodes).\n\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If any starting node doesn't exist in the graph.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nRandomized greedy fills a set by visiting the nodes in a random order, adding each\nnode that is not yet excluded, and excluding its neighborhood from further\nconsideration. This takes $O(V + E)$ time with $O(V)$ auxiliary memory, and the\nresult is guaranteed *maximal* (no further node can be added) but not necessarily\n*maximum*, which is $\\mathsf{NP}$-hard to compute exactly. Supplying `seed=` locks\nreproducibility; passing `nodes=` fixes a starting independent set that the\nalgorithm then extends. For the largest possible set, `maximum_independent_set()`\nruns a different (still greedy but degree-aware) heuristic.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~maximum_independent_set`) \n\n:   Look for the largest independent set instead.\n\n[](`~find_maximal_independent_sets`) \n\n:   Draw many maximal independent sets at once.\n\n[](`~is_independent_set`) \n\n:   Check a set of nodes.\n\n[](`~dominating_set`) \n\n:   Every maximal independent set is also dominating.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nOn a path of five nodes, a maximal independent set has either two nodes, such as `B` and\n`D`, or three, `A`, `C` and `E`; which one comes back depends on the seed:\n\n::: {#0fe5a25a .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"E\")]\n)\n\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\n\n[\n    [label(n) for n in graph.maximal_independent_set(seed=s)]\n    for s in range(3)\n]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[['A', 'C', 'E'], ['B', 'D'], ['A', 'D']]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Filling a slot\n\n`exams()` joins two of the 14 courses at a made-up college when some student takes both,\nso their exams can't share a time slot. Starting from Calculus, the method adds exams\nuntil no other can join without a conflict, which gives a slot for Calculus and four\nmore exams:\n\n::: {#002496aa .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams = ec.exams()\ncalculus = exams.get_id_from_label(\"Calculus\")\nslot = exams.maximal_independent_set(nodes=[calculus], seed=1)\n\n[exams.get_label_from_id(n) for n in slot]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n['Calculus', 'History', 'Literature', 'Databases', 'Web Design']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#113f03c9 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams.plot(highlight=slot, label_by=\"label\", seed=5)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c3